<Layout track="space" section="curriculum" title="Space technology curriculum & tools" description="Eight space systems modules covering mission analysis, spacecraft subsystems, telemetry, autonomy and an educational capstone, with tools and review criteria.">
<PageHero slot="hero" eyebrow="SPACE TECHNOLOGY / CURRICULUM" title="Think in systems. Learn by testing." description="Eight connected modules turn a mission idea into a reviewable engineering package. Explore the concepts, tools, practical work and evidence at each step." image="space-tech" imageAlt="AI-generated learning scene with learners collaborating; not actual academy participants" />
<section class="container section"><p class="eyebrow">PLANNED LEARNING MODULES</p><h2>The engineering behind the mission.</h2><p class="lede">Tool choices support the learning task. Optional hardware work depends on agreed facilities and supervision; simulation remains the common starting point.</p><ModuleExplorer modules={space.modules} label="Space technology"/></section>
<section class="container section section-tight two-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">A SMALL EXAMPLE. AN IMPORTANT DISTINCTION.</p><h2>Power is not energy.</h2><p>A payload drawing 8 W for 25% of an operating cycle contributes 2 W average demand. Add a continuously running 2 W computer and a 10 W transmitter operating 10% of the time: the average is 5 W before losses.</p><p>If all three operate together, the instantaneous demand is 20 W. A design review must preserve both numbers and assess eclipse energy separately. This is an illustrative calculation, not a component specification.</p></div><div class="readiness-card"><h3>Practice explaining the tradeoff</h3><p>What changes when the payload runs longer? Which subsystem must respond? How will you show that your assumptions still hold?</p><p>Work towards a budget another team member can inspect and reproduce.</p></div></section>
<section class="container section section-tight"><h2>Explore the wider discipline.</h2><p>Optional background reading: <a class="text-link" href="https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/systems-engineering-series/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA’s systems engineering series</a> and <a class="text-link" href="https://www.esa.int/Education/CanSat" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ESA’s CanSat education resources</a>. These are independent learning references; no institutional affiliation is implied.</p></section><ProgramEnd track="space" /></Layout>